       finally:\n            record.message = full_message\n        ...\n```\nBut super().format reassigns record.message = record.getMessage() = full message, ignoring our truncated. So no.\n\nSo implement own:\n\n```python\n        record.message = message\n        try:\n            if self.usesTime():\n                record.asctime = self.formatTime(record, self.datefmt)\n            s = self.formatMessage(record)\n            if record.exc_info:\n                if not record.exc_text:\n                    record.exc_text = self.formatException(record.exc_info)\n            if record.exc_text:\n                if s[-1:] != \"\\n\":\n                    s = s + \"\\n\"\n                s = s + record.exc_text\n            if record.stack_info:\n                if s[-1:] != \"\\n\":\n                    s = s + \"\\n\"\n                s = s + self.formatStack(record.stack_info)\n        finally:\n            record.message = full_message\n```\nThat replicates stdlib. Then apply newline prefix logic and color.\n\nHmm but \"沿用原有多行前缀、时间精度、颜色及异常处理\" — yes.\n\nAlternatively call super().format but temporarily patch formatMessage? Could use:\n\n```python\n        original_format_message = self.formatMessage\n```\nNo, simpler to replicate. But risk: stdlib version differences. Python version here likely 3.12/3.13. Let me check the Formatter.format source in this env to replicate exactly.\n\nActually another approach that reuses super().format: temporarily set record.message via a subclass trick... We could create a proxy record using `logging.LogRecord` copy: `copy.copy` not in imports; but we can use `_log_record` class? Hmm.\n\nAlternative clean approach: use a lightweight wrapper object copying attributes:\n\n```python\nclass _RecordProxy: ...\n```\nNot needed. Replicating is fine; I'll check stdlib source.\n\nTruncate function:\n\n```python\n_MESSAGE_OMITTED = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\ndef _truncate_message(message, budget):\n    if len(message) <= budget:\n        re